@topic backend.overview
title: The four backends
summary: interp, compile, cranelift-generic and cranelift implement the same semantics; they differ in speed, in where they run, and in the native-only operations only the native backends perform.
index: backends
alias: backends; which backend; backend comparison
keywords: interp, compile, cranelift, native, JIT, Tcl, speed, semantics
related: backend.interp backend.compile backend.cranelift backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-executable backend.native-only backend.io backend.performance task.choose-backend cli.options
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md

== WHAT THEY ARE
    interp             the reference interpreter (the default): runs the
                       core IR the program's HIR lowers to; the executable
                       specification, deliberately simple and slow
    compile            compiles the program's HIR to Tcl procedures and
                       runs them: the same semantics, much faster than interp
    cranelift          compiles the HIR to native machine code with
                       Cranelift (JIT) after call-site specialization
    cranelift-generic  the same native backend without specialization
Select one with -backend NAME. `-emit-native-executable` uses the native
backend to build a standalone program (backend.native-executable).

== WHAT IS THE SAME
The language: values, errors, proofs and every compile-time diagnostic and
warning are decided before any backend runs, from the same analyzed HIR, so
a program is accepted or rejected identically everywhere. A program that
runs on two backends computes the same value and fails with the same error
code (the test suites check this across all four).

== WHAT DIFFERS
  * Speed: interp is the slowest by far (backend.performance).
  * Native-only operations: printing through io, raw system calls, machine
    addresses and C calls exist only on the native backends; interp and
    compile refuse them at run time with NATIVE-ONLY (backend.native-only,
    backend.io).
  * Availability: the native backends need the native executables (built in
    a checkout with cargo; packed in the single executable) and a supported
    platform (backend.platforms).
  * Input files: a .ir file (core IR text) runs on interp and compile only.

@topic backend.interp
title: interp: the reference interpreter
summary: The default backend: a tree-walking interpreter of the core IR the program lowers to; the executable specification of the semantics, and the slowest backend.
index: backends
alias: interp; interpreter; reference interpreter
keywords: interp, interpreter, reference, core IR, recursion limit
related: backend.overview backend.compile backend.performance
docs: README.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend interp` (also the default). The front end lowers the analyzed HIR
to core IR, and core/evaluator.tcl walks it. It aims for clarity over
speed: it is the reference the other backends are tested against.

== THINGS TO KNOW
  * It is the slowest backend, often a hundred times slower than the native
    ones on search-heavy code; use `compile` or a native backend for real
    workloads, and keep tests that must also run here small.
  * Deep recursion works thousands of calls deep (the compiler raises Tcl's
    recursion limit for programs).
  * It refuses native-only operations (io printing, syscalls, C calls) with
    NATIVE-ONLY when they run.

@topic backend.compile
title: compile: the Tcl compiler backend
summary: Compiles the analyzed HIR to Tcl procedures and runs them: the same semantics as interp, many times faster, on any platform Tcl 9 runs on.
index: backends
alias: compile backend; tcl compiler
keywords: compile, Tcl, procedures, -code
related: backend.overview backend.interp backend.performance cli.options
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend compile`. compiler/compiler.tcl compiles the program's HIR once
into Tcl code (shown by -code) and runs it. With -backend compile, the
compiler also prints the inferred types of the program's top-level bindings.

== THINGS TO KNOW
Like interp it refuses native-only operations with NATIVE-ONLY. It is the
fastest backend that needs nothing but Tcl.

@topic backend.cranelift
title: cranelift: native code, specialized
summary: Compiles the program to machine code with Cranelift, specializing function instances by the argument kinds their call sites prove; prints the program's output as it runs.
index: backends
alias: cranelift; native backend; JIT; machine code
keywords: cranelift, native, JIT, specialization, NIR, CLIF, botlish-native
related: backend.overview backend.cranelift-generic backend.native-executable backend.native-only backend.platforms concept.specialization
docs: README.md DIRECT-HIR-NATIVE-PATH.md NATIVE-AUDIT.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend cranelift`. native/lower.tcl lowers the analyzed HIR to NIR (the
native IR, shown by -emit-nir), the Rust driver botlish-native translates it
to Cranelift IR (-emit-clif) and runs the machine code against the native
runtime. Function instances are specialized by the argument kinds their
call sites prove (concept.specialization).

== THINGS TO KNOW
  * It performs the native-only operations: io printing, Linux system
    calls, C calls (backend.native-only). What the program prints appears
    on standard output and error as it runs, before the compiler's `value:`
    line.
  * It needs the native executables: in a checkout, `cargo build --release
    --manifest-path native/Cargo.toml` (otherwise NATIVE NOT-BUILT); the
    single executable packs them.
  * A construct the native lowering does not support fails with NATIVE
    UNSUPPORTED; native stack exhaustion is NATIVE LIMIT STACK.

@topic backend.cranelift-generic
title: cranelift-generic: native code, unspecialized
summary: The native backend without call-site specialization: one guarded, generic instance per function; the baseline the specialized backend is measured against.
index: backends
alias: cranelift-generic; generic native
keywords: generic, unspecialized, native, guards
related: backend.cranelift backend.overview
docs: README.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-backend cranelift-generic`: the same pipeline as cranelift with
specialization off (BOTLISH_NATIVE_SPECIALIZE=0 has the same effect on
-emit-nir and -emit-clif). It runs the same programs with the same results;
it exists as a baseline and a cross-check, and on some programs compiles
faster.

@topic backend.native-executable
title: Standalone native executables
summary: -emit-native-executable FILE.bot compiles the program into a standalone executable (ELF, Mach-O or PE) that runs without Tcl, Rust or a checkout and prints its output, then its value.
index: backends
alias: native executable; standalone executable; emit-native-executable; compile to binary
keywords: executable, ELF, Mach-O, PE, exe, AOT, stub linker, rustc, emit, macOS, Windows, MSVC
related: backend.cranelift backend.single-executable backend.platforms cli.exit-status concept.argv
docs: README.md SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md ARGV.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-5.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-emit-native-executable game.bot` writes ./game (the input's name without
its extension; game.exe on Windows) and does not run the program. The
executable prints what the program prints, then the program's final value
on its own line (`unit` for a program ending in unit), and exits 0; a
run-time error goes to stderr with a nonzero exit status. Its argv() is its
real process arguments. It is an
ordinary command-line program of the host: an ELF executable linked
against glibc on Linux, a Mach-O arm64 executable linked against libSystem
(and signed ad hoc by the linker, as every arm64 executable is) on macOS, a
PE x86-64 console executable linked by Microsoft's link.exe, with the C
runtime linked into it, on Windows. It needs no source file, Botlish
checkout or compiler to run, only the system's own libraries (on Windows
no Visual C++ runtime either).

== REQUIREMENTS
Every function instance the program uses must be closed (fully known to the
ahead-of-time compiler); otherwise NATIVE AOT NOT-READY names the cause
(-aot-spec explains). A .ir file is rejected (NATIVE AOT INPUT): executables
are built from .bot, .hir or .hirx. Linking (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER): in a
checkout the default linker uses rustc and the system C linker (rustc), on
Linux x86-64 and AArch64, on macOS AArch64 and on Windows x86-64 (MSVC's
toolchain: Visual Studio's C++ build tools and the Windows SDK, which rustc
finds itself); the `system` linker needs no rustc -- the host's C compiler
driver (cc; on Windows link.exe from a Developer Command Prompt) links the
program with the precompiled runtime archive (libbotlish_runtime.a, beside
the driver or BOTLISH_NATIVE_RUNTIME), on the same hosts; the `stub` linker
needs no toolchain and is Linux x86-64 only; `auto` (what the single
executable uses) is the system linker for a program that calls C and the
stub for any other. A program that calls C links with the rustc and the
system linker, its declared libraries passed to the platform linker, and
the stub linker never (NATIVE LINK). The
executable's machine code uses
the target's baseline instruction set, whichever native backend the
command line names: it is always the closed, specialized program.

@topic backend.native-library
title: Native libraries: Botlish functions a C host calls
summary: `exported c fn` declares a C export; -emit-native-library NAME builds libNAME.a, libNAME.so, NAME.h and a manifest that any C host can link and call.
index: backends
alias: native library; library mode; exported c fn; emit-native-library; static library; shared library; C export; call Botlish from C
keywords: exported, export, library, .a, .so, header, manifest, instance, create, destroy, thread, C ABI, int64_t, owned buffer, Tcl extension, embedding
related: backend.native-executable concept.c-ffi cli.options error.export-signature error.export-placement error.export-name error.export-environment error.native-library
docs: NATIVE-LIBRARY-MILESTONE-1.md C-FFI.md

== WHAT IT IS
A Botlish program can be a library a C program calls. Its exports are
declared in the source:

    error Rejected:
        code: int

    exported c fn checked(n: int) -> int errors Rejected:
        if n < 0:
            fail Rejected {code: n}
        n * 2

and `-emit-native-library NAME FILE.bot` builds (Linux glibc, x86_64 and
aarch64):

    libNAME.a            a static library: one object with the program and
                         a private copy of the runtime; only the library's
                         public C functions are global symbols
    libNAME.so           the same as a shared library
    NAME.h               the C interface
    NAME.manifest.json   the same interface as data, for tools

C calls `NAME_checked(instance, int64_t n, int64_t *result, NAME_error
**error)`. An exported c fn is an ordinary function to Botlish code and to
every backend; only a library makes a C symbol of it.

== THE C INTERFACE
  * Lifecycle: NAME_create makes an instance on the calling thread and runs
    the program's initialization (its modules' static initialization); the
    instance is called and destroyed (NAME_destroy) on that thread only,
    one call at a time. Instances share nothing; two libraries in one
    process share nothing but the C library.
  * Statuses: every call returns NAME_STATUS_OK, DECLARED (a declared
    Botlish error), RUNTIME, BOUNDARY (an argument or result C cannot
    carry), INVOCATION (a handle, thread or pointer the rules forbid) or
    INTERNAL. On failure the optional error out-parameter receives an error
    the caller owns: its identity (the canonical error name, e.g.
    "Rejected" or "module::Malformed", or a runtime error's code), its
    message, and its payload's int, bool, Float64 and str fields.
  * Types: int is int64_t (an Int result outside it is a BOUNDARY error,
    never wrapped), bool is bool, Float64 is double (NaN and the
    infinities are refused), str is UTF-8 -- a pointer and a length in, an
    owned NAME_bytes buffer out, released with NAME_bytes_release.

== THE EXPORT ROOT
An exported function is compiled for every argument its declared types
admit, never for what its Botlish callers happen to pass: Botlish calls of
it keep their own specialized code, and the export is a separate entry. A
library's entry file holds declarations only (NATIVE LIBRARY
LIBRARY-TOP-LEVEL), so no entry script runs when an instance is created.

== THREE DIFFERENT THINGS
  * A module's top-level functions are visible to every importer: Botlish
    has no visibility modifiers or export lists (capability.visibility).
    That is Botlish-to-Botlish reach, through the Botlish calling
    convention, and it makes no C symbol.
  * `extern c fn` declares a C function Botlish calls (concept.c-ffi): a
    foreign import, with no body.
  * `exported c fn` gives a Botlish function a C entry point a host calls:
    a foreign export. It changes nothing for Botlish callers, which still
    call it directly; only -emit-native-library reads it. The explicit
    `-export FUNCTION` list (native::library -exports) selects top-level
    functions of the entry program the same way, checked by the same rules.

== LIMITATIONS
Botlish code runs on the caller's thread and stack, and no stack guard is
installed in a library: deep recursion needs a large thread stack, and
exhausting it ends the process. No callbacks, no Lists, structs or other
values at the boundary, no asynchronous calls; macOS and Windows libraries
are not made yet.

@topic backend.single-executable
title: The single-file compiler
summary: The packed `botlish` executable is this compiler, its command line, the standard library, Compilerpedia and both native executables in one Linux x86-64 file that needs only glibc.
index: backends
alias: single executable; botlish executable; packed compiler; zipkit
keywords: single, zipkit, zipfs, cache, install, glibc
related: backend.native-executable backend.platforms cli.usage
docs: SINGLE-EXECUTABLE.md

== WHAT IT IS
A Tcl 9 zipkit (single/build.sh in a checkout): a static tclsh with the
compiler and main.tcl in an attached archive. It takes main.tcl's options,
runs every backend, builds standalone executables, and answers -help from
the packed Compilerpedia, with no source tree present. It links a program
that calls no C with the stub linker, which needs no toolchain, and one that
calls C (or declares a C library) with the system linker: the host's C
compiler driver (cc, with its linker) and the runtime archive the compiler
carries -- never Rust or a checkout (BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=auto).

== THINGS TO KNOW
On its first run that needs a native backend it copies its two native
executables into a cache directory ($BOTLISH_CACHE_DIR,
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/botlish or ~/.cache/botlish), and on its first run that may
link (-emit-native-executable, a native backend) its runtime archive too;
without a usable cache it uses a temporary one. Usage, -help and -version
need no installation. It is built for Linux x86-64. A program that calls C
needs a C compiler on the machine that builds it (Debian, Ubuntu: apt
install gcc) and the libraries it declares; without one its link says so
(NATIVE LINK TOOLCHAIN). The executable it builds needs neither.

@topic backend.native-only
title: Native-only operations
summary: Machine addresses, raw Linux system calls and C calls exist only on the native backends; interp and compile accept such programs but refuse the operation with NATIVE-ONLY when it runs.
index: backends rules
alias: native only; native-only operations; only on cranelift
keywords: NATIVE-ONLY, syscall, address, io, ffi, C, Tcl backends
related: backend.io backend.overview concept.c-ffi concept.direct-modules concept.context-traits
docs: LINUX-X86-64-SYSCALL.md ABI-BYTES.md C-FFI.md CONTEXT-TRAITS.md

== WHAT THEY ARE
  * the address bridges std::abi::x86_64::from_bytes / from_mutable_bytes
    (and the aarch64 ones): a byte buffer's machine address;
  * the system-call transports std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall and
    std::linux::abi::aarch64::syscall;
  * calls of C functions (`extern c fn`, C-FFI.md);
and so everything built on them: stdlib/linux.bot, std::linux::io, and
therefore io's printing through std::linux::io::LinuxIO. Each such
standard-library entry says so in its BACKENDS section (-help
std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall).

== WHY
The Tcl backends have no machine addresses and do not imitate the kernel:
pretending would make them disagree with the native backends about what a
program does. So they compile such a program like any other and stop at the
operation with a clear run-time error (CORE SEMANTIC NATIVE-ONLY).

== WHAT TO DO
Run the program with -backend cranelift (or cranelift-generic), or build an
executable. For a program that must run everywhere, keep the native-only
part at the edge: compute a value in portable code and print it only in a
native entry program, or return the result as the program's value, which
every backend prints (backend.io).

@topic backend.io
title: Which forms of I/O work on which backend
summary: The program's value is printed on every backend; printing (std::io::print_line) and reading stdin need a native backend or an executable; there is no portable file or exit-status API.
index: backends stdlib
alias: input output; io backends; printing on interp; output on every backend
keywords: io, print, print_line, stdout, stderr, value, native, interp
related: task.print-output backend.native-only stdlib.module.std::io stdlib.std::io::print_line stdlib.module.std::darwin::io stdlib.module.std::windows::io capability.input capability.files capability.process-exit-status concept.program-value concept.context-traits
docs: CONTEXT-TRAITS.md POST-2048-PRODUCT-FIXES.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-8.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md

== OUTPUT, BY BACKEND
    the program's value          every backend: the compiler prints it
                                 after "value:"; an executable prints it last
    std::io::print, print_line,  cranelift, cranelift-generic and native
    write_error(_line)           executables; interp and compile refuse the
                                 underlying system call (NATIVE-ONLY)
    std::linux::write,           the same: native only
    std::linux::io
    std::darwin::write,          the same: native only (C calls)
    std::darwin::io
    std::windows::write_file,    the same: native only (C calls)
    std::windows::io

io's operations need an installed context implementing std::io::IO; the
standard library provides three, one per platform: std::linux::io::LinuxIO
(`with context std::linux::io::create()`), which makes raw Linux system
calls; on macOS std::darwin::io::DarwinIO (`with context
std::darwin::io::create()`), which calls libSystem's write(2) through C; and
on Windows std::windows::io::WindowsIO (`with context
std::windows::io::create()`), which calls kernel32's GetStdHandle and
WriteFile through C (the text's UTF-8 bytes, exactly: no code page, no
newline translation) -- hence native only. The function that prints is the
same on all three; only the installed context differs.

== INPUT
    argv()                       every backend (concept.argv)
    std::linux::io::read(buffer)      standard input, raw bytes: cranelift,
    std::darwin::io::read(buffer)     cranelift-generic and native executables
                                 (capability.input)

There is no text or line input in io, and no portable file or directory API
(capability.files).

== EXIT STATUS
A program cannot set its process exit status
(capability.process-exit-status).

@topic backend.platforms
title: Platforms and what runs where
summary: The Tcl backends need only Tcl 9; the native backend runs on Linux, macOS AArch64 and Windows x86-64, C calls too (variadic ones not on Windows); syscalls are Linux's; stub linking Linux x86-64's.
index: backends
alias: platforms; operating systems; aarch64; arm64; x86-64; supported platforms
keywords: Linux, x86-64, AArch64, arm64, macOS, Windows, MSVC, Tcl 9, coroutines, stub
related: backend.targets backend.overview backend.native-executable backend.single-executable
docs: README.md LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md COROUTINES.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-1.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-3.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-8.md

== WHAT IS TESTED
The repository's continuous integration runs every test on Linux x86-64 and
Linux AArch64 (the Tcl backends, the native backends, the golden suites)
and builds the single executable on Linux x86-64. A separate workflow runs
the native JIT, native coroutines, the native stack guard, standalone
executables, C calls and Darwin I/O on Apple Silicon macOS, and
another the native JIT, standalone executables, C calls (against
libraries MSVC's cl builds), Windows I/O, the collector under GC stress
(its roots found in native frames, checked against Windows' own
unwinder), and every Tcl test file and golden suite on the Tcl backends,
on Windows x86-64 with MSVC's toolchain. Nothing here is a promise about
other platforms.

== PER COMPONENT
    interp, compile          Tcl 9.0 or newer (Unicode-correct strings);
                             no machine-specific code
    cranelift(-generic)      the native executables (Rust, Cranelift);
                             Linux x86-64 and AArch64, macOS AArch64,
                             Windows x86-64 (MSVC's toolchain; MinGW's
                             windows-x86_64-gnu is no native target)
    native coroutines        Linux x86-64 and AArch64 and macOS AArch64
                             (hand-written stack switching, or, for a
                             coroutine closed inside one function, none:
                             COROUTINE-FUSION-MILESTONE-1.md); elsewhere
                             (Windows) NATIVE UNSUPPORTED; the Tcl
                             backends run coroutines everywhere
    native stack overflow    NATIVE LIMIT STACK, for the thread's stack
                             and a coroutine's, on Linux x86-64 and
                             AArch64 and macOS AArch64; on Windows only
                             for Botlish recursion the stack-limit check
                             stops, otherwise the process ends
                             (error.native-limit-stack)
    Linux system calls, io   Linux; x86-64 through std::linux::abi::x86_64,
                             AArch64 through std::linux::abi::aarch64 (one
                             portable surface: linux, std::linux::io, io);
                             the linux namespaces compile for Linux
                             targets only (error.unsupported-platform)
    Darwin I/O, io           macOS AArch64: libSystem's read, write,
                             open, close and errno through C calls
                             (darwin, std::darwin::io, std::darwin::path; no raw
                             Darwin system call); the darwin namespaces
                             compile for macOS targets only
    Windows I/O, io          Windows x86-64: kernel32's GetStdHandle,
                             WriteFile and GetLastError through C calls
                             (windows, std::windows::io: standard output and
                             error only); the windows namespaces compile
                             for Windows targets only
    executables              Linux, glibc, ELF: rustc linker on x86-64
                             and AArch64, stub linker x86-64 only; macOS
                             AArch64, Mach-O: rustc linker; Windows
                             x86-64, PE (NAME.exe): rustc and link.exe
    C calls (extern c fn)    Linux, glibc (GNU ld's link plan, the SysV
                             and AAPCS64 C ABIs), macOS AArch64 (Apple's
                             ld, Apple's arm64 C ABI) and Windows x86-64
                             (link.exe: /LIBPATH and NAME.lib, the Win64
                             C ABI, the static C runtime); variadic C
                             calls not on Windows (NATIVE CODEGEN);
                             never with the stub linker
    single executable        Linux x86-64

@topic backend.targets
title: -target: the compilation target, an operating system and an architecture
summary: -target NAME picks the operating system and architecture a program is built for (C type sizes, modules, platforms); native code runs only for the host's own target.
index: backends
alias: target; -target; cross compile; C ABI target
keywords: target, linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos, macos-aarch64, windows, windows-x86_64-gnu, mingw, ffi, std::linux::sys, architecture, operating system, triple
related: backend.platforms concept.c-ffi cli.options error.unsupported-platform error.native-target error.hir-interchange-target
docs: C-ABI-TYPES.md LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md MACOS-AARCH64-MILESTONE-2.md

== WHAT IT DOES
The targets are linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos-x86_64, macos-aarch64,
windows-x86_64, windows-x86_64-gnu and windows-aarch64 (to the native
backend, triples: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, ..., aarch64-apple-darwin); the
default is the host's. A target is an operating system and an architecture:
linux-aarch64 and macos-aarch64 share an instruction set, not a target. On
Windows the C toolchain's ABI family is part of the target too:
windows-x86_64 is MSVC's (x86_64-pc-windows-msvc), windows-x86_64-gnu
MinGW-w64's (x86_64-pc-windows-gnu), the target of the generated Win32
bindings (concept.c-bindgen-layouts).
The target decides:
  * the widths and signedness of std::ffi::c's C integer types
    (std::ffi::c::Long is 64 bits on Linux and macOS, 32 on Windows; plain
    std::ffi::c::Char is unsigned on Linux AArch64 only);
  * which file an architecture-specific module comes from (std::linux::sys
    is stdlib/linux/sys.x86_64.bot or stdlib/linux/sys.aarch64.bot);
  * whether a platform namespace is available: linux and everything
    under it exist for Linux targets only, darwin for macOS's and windows
    for Windows' (error.unsupported-platform);
  * the C calling convention of foreign calls (Apple's on macOS AArch64).
A HIR built with any of these records its target, and is never taken for
another's: a .hirx file of another target is refused
(error.hir-interchange-target), and the front-end cache keeps one entry
per target.

== WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
It is not cross-compilation to machine code: the native backends run and
emit code only for the host's own target, and refuse another target's
program (error.native-target). The Tcl backends run a program built for
any target. An unknown name is a usage error listing the targets.

@topic backend.hir-interchange
title: HIR interchange (.hirx) and the front-end cache
summary: -emit-hir-interchange writes a program's analyzed HIR as FILE.hirx, which any backend runs without the front end; the same format backs the opt-in front-end cache (BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE).
index: backends
alias: hirx; hir interchange; front-end cache; BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE
keywords: hirx, cache, interchange, fingerprint, front end
related: cli.options backend.overview
docs: HIR-INTERCHANGE.md

== WHAT IT IS
`-emit-hir-interchange game.bot` writes game.hirx and runs nothing;
`botlish game.hirx` (any backend) runs it with no parsing or analysis. A
.hirx file is valid only for the front end that wrote it: another one
rejects it with HIR INTERCHANGE COMPILER (the front end's identity is the
"front end" fingerprint -version prints). With BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE=DIR, a .bot
program's analyzed HIR is kept in DIR and reused while the program, its
modules and the front end are unchanged.

@topic backend.performance
title: How fast each backend is
summary: interp is the reference and the slowest; compile is many times faster; the native backends are fastest at run time but pay a fixed compilation cost per run.
index: backends
alias: performance; speed; slow interpreter; which backend is fastest
keywords: speed, slow, fast, benchmark, compile time
related: backend.overview task.choose-backend backend.native-executable

== ROUGH ORDERS OF MAGNITUDE
Measured on a 15-puzzle solver's IDA* search (an application study): interp
about 2.5 minutes, compile about 5 seconds, the native backends about 1.3 to
1.5 seconds -- most of which was fixed front-end and JIT cost; a standalone
executable ran the same workload's search in about 2 seconds of a longer
benchmark. Front-end time (parsing and analysis) is the same for every
backend and dominates short programs: a 600-line test suite took several
seconds to compile. These are observations, not guarantees.

== WHAT TO DO
Develop and test on the native backends or compile; keep inputs of tests
that must also pass on interp small; build an executable for repeated runs
(it pays no compilation per run).

