== FORMS
    botlish FILE.bot                run the program on interp
    botlish -backend cranelift FILE.bot
    botlish -emit-native-executable FILE.bot     build ./FILE
    botlish -check FILE.bot         report its diagnostics, run nothing
    botlish -lsp                    the language server, for an editor
    botlish -help [QUERY...]        Compilerpedia
    botlish -version                the build identity
    botlish                         the usage summary (exit 0)
In a repository checkout the compiler is `tclsh9.0 main.tcl` with the same
arguments. Running it with no arguments only prints usage: it runs no
program.

== INPUT FILES
    FILE.bot    Botlish source; its imports name standard namespaces and
                the .bot files beside it (concept.modules)
    FILE.hir    HIR text
    FILE.hirx   HIR interchange (backend.hir-interchange)
    FILE.ir     core IR text: interp and compile only
Several files are run one after another, each with its own "== FILE"
header.

@topic cli.options
title: Every command-line option
summary: The options of botlish (tclsh9.0 main.tcl): backend and warning selection, program arguments, building, checking, the language server, inspection output, and the reference.
index: backends
alias: options; flags of the command line; command-line options
keywords: -backend, -warnings, -target, -L, -argv, -argv-hex, -argv-none, -emit-native-executable, -emit-hir-interchange, -emit-native-library, -export, -out, -check, -lsp, -lsp-scheduler, -lsp-worker, -code, -hir, -ast, -aot, -aot-data, -aot-spec, -contexts, -direct, -traits, -emit-nir, -emit-clif, -help, -version
related: cli.usage cli.output cli.exit-status cli.lsp lint.policy backend.targets backend.hir-interchange backend.native-executable backend.native-library concept.argv
docs: README.md

== RUNNING
    -backend NAME     interp (default), compile, cranelift,
                      cranelift-generic (backend.overview)
    -warnings MODE    default (print warnings, run), off (no warning
                      pass), error (reject a program that has a warning)
                      (lint.policy)
    -target NAME      the target (operating system and architecture) a
                      .bot program is compiled, or a .hirx file read, for:
                      linux-x86_64, linux-aarch64, macos-x86_64,
                      macos-aarch64, windows-x86_64, windows-x86_64-gnu,
                      windows-aarch64; default the host's
                      (backend.targets)
    -L DIR            add DIR to the system linker's library search path,
                      for a program that calls C (concept.c-ffi)

== PROGRAM ARGUMENTS (argv())
    -argv TEXT        append one argument (its UTF-8 bytes); the first one
                      given is argument zero
    -argv-hex HEX     append one argument given as the hex of its raw bytes
                      (so invalid UTF-8 is expressible; "" is an empty one)
    -argv-none        the empty argument vector
Without any of them argv() is the one-element default ["botlish-runner"],
never the compiler's own arguments. An executable ignores them: it reads its
own process arguments.

== BUILDING (nothing runs)
    -emit-native-executable   a standalone executable beside each input
                              (backend.native-executable)
    -emit-hir-interchange     each input's analyzed HIR as FILE.hirx
                              (backend.hir-interchange)
    -emit-native-library NAME the native library NAME from the one input:
                              libNAME.a, libNAME.so, NAME.h and
                              NAME.manifest.json (backend.native-library)
    -export FUNCTION          export the entry program's top-level FUNCTION
                              (repeatable; without it the library exports
                              the program's `exported c fn`s)
    -out DIR                  where -emit-native-library writes (default:
                              the input's directory)

== CHECKING (nothing runs, nothing is built or written)
    -check            analyze each .bot input and report what compiling it
                      reports: the same error lines (and help lines), its
                      warnings on stderr, "ok" when it has no error; exit
                      status 1 when an input has one. Past a syntax error
                      it also reports the semantic errors the malformed
                      source cannot affect, which a run does not
                      (error.surface-syntax). Only -warnings and
                      -target combine with it (they decide the analysis);
                      any other option, or an input that is not a .bot
                      file, is a malformed invocation.

== LANGUAGE SERVER (cli.lsp; no input file)
    -lsp              run the language server: the Language Server
                      Protocol on stdin and stdout, for an editor. It
                      analyzes what the editor opens exactly as -check
                      does, runs and builds nothing, and takes only
                      -warnings, -target and -lsp-scheduler.
    -lsp-scheduler NAME   where -lsp analyzes: worker (the default: a
                      persistent worker process, so the server keeps
                      answering while an analysis runs) or sync (in the
                      server's own process, the reference design)
    -lsp-worker       internal: the analysis worker -lsp starts itself
                      (a private protocol on stdin and stdout); not for
                      direct use

== INSPECTING (printed before the program runs)
    -code             the Tcl code the compile backend generates
    -hir              the program's semantic HIR
    -ast              a .bot file's surface syntax tree
    -aot              every function's closed-AOT readiness
    -aot-data         the same analysis as data
    -aot-spec         the AOT report of every specialized function instance
    -contexts         the context structs, installations and every
                      function's context requirements, with the chain that
                      explains each
    -direct           each module's directness (concept.direct-modules)
    -traits           traits, conformances and specializations
    -emit-nir         the native IR (NIR) of the program
    -emit-clif        the Cranelift IR of every function

== REFERENCE
    -help [QUERY...]  Compilerpedia: alone, its indices; with a query, the
                      topic or the matching topics. Every argument after
                      -help is part of the query. --help and -h are the same.
    -version          the build identity (--version is the same)

== ENVIRONMENT
    BOTLISH_WARNINGS=default|off|error   the warning mode of a compilation
                                         given no -warnings
    BOTLISH_HIR_CACHE=DIR                the front-end cache
    BOTLISH_NATIVE_LINKER=rustc|stub     how executables are linked
    BOTLISH_LSP_STATS=FILE               -lsp writes its measurements to
                                         FILE when it exits

== MALFORMED INVOCATIONS
An unknown option, an option without its argument (`-backend` last), an
unknown backend, mode or target, a malformed -argv-hex, -check beside an
option it does not take or with an input that is not Botlish source, -lsp
beside an option it does not take or with an input file, an unknown
-lsp-scheduler, or options without an input file print a message on stderr
and exit with status 2; nothing is compiled.

@topic cli.output
title: What a run prints
summary: Per file: "== FILE (BACKEND)", the program's output (native backends), "   value: V"; or "   error:" lines, each with a "   help:" line naming its topic; warnings go to stderr.
index: backends
alias: output format; value line; what main prints
keywords: value, ==, error, help, warning, stderr, stdout, refinements
related: cli.usage cli.exit-status concept.program-value backend.io lint.policy
docs: README.md

== A RUN
    == game.bot (cranelift)
    ...what the program printed (native backends)...
       value: 42
The value is the program's last expression, shown as a literal ("text",
[1, 2], {a: 1}, enum cases by name). With -backend compile the inferred
types of top-level bindings follow ("   type:  name : T"). A Block value
(a function) is followed by the refinements visible in its environment.

== A REJECTED PROGRAM
    == broken.bot (interp)
       error: broken.bot:3:5: unbound name "x" (CORE SEMANTIC UNBOUND)
       help: botlish -help error.unbound
Every independent diagnostic gets its own line (DIAGNOSTIC-RECOVERY.md);
each is followed by a help line naming the Compilerpedia topic of its rule
or code, once per topic per file.

== WARNINGS
Printed on stderr as they are found, `FILE:LINE:COL: warning: MESSAGE
(CODE)` with `note:` lines for related locations, then one `help:` line per
distinct code. Standard output stays the program's.

== A CHECK
    == game.bot (check)
       ok
With -check (cli.options) the header names no backend and nothing runs: a
program without an error gets "ok"; a rejected one the same "error:" and
"help:" lines a run prints, and its warnings go to stderr as a run's do.
One difference: a program with a syntax error gets its syntax errors and
then the independent semantic errors of the code around them, where a run
stops at the syntax errors (error.surface-syntax, "Checking around a
syntax error").

@topic cli.exit-status
title: Exit statuses of the compiler and of executables
summary: botlish exits 0 when every input compiled and ran, 1 when any failed, 2 for a malformed invocation; -help exits 1 when nothing matches; an executable exits 0 or, after a run-time error, nonzero.
index: backends
alias: exit code; exit codes; compiler exit status
keywords: exit, status, 0, 1, 2, failure
related: cli.usage capability.process-exit-status backend.native-executable

== THE COMPILER
    0   every input compiled and ran (or was built, or checked without an
        error), or usage, -help with a match, -version
    1   some input was rejected or failed at run time; or -help found no
        topic (near matches on stderr)
    2   a malformed invocation (stderr says what)
With -lsp: 0 when the editor ended the session in order (shutdown, then
exit), 1 otherwise (exit without shutdown, the end of its input, input that
cannot be read as protocol messages).

== A STANDALONE EXECUTABLE
0 after printing the program's value; nonzero after a run-time error (on
stderr). The program cannot choose its own exit status
(capability.process-exit-status).

@topic cli.lsp
title: The language server (editor integration)
summary: botlish -lsp is a language server on stdin and stdout: the compiler's own diagnostics, hover, definitions and references for an editor's unsaved Botlish source.
index: backends
alias: lsp; language server; editor; neovim; vscode; ide; editor support
keywords: -lsp, LSP, hover, definition, references, diagnostics, editor, neovim
related: cli.options cli.usage cli.exit-status concept.modules
docs: LSP-MILESTONE-1.md COMPILER-SERVICE-MILESTONE-1.md

== STARTING IT
    botlish -lsp                    (tclsh9.0 main.tcl -lsp in a checkout)
An editor starts the server and talks to it on its stdin and stdout; the
server writes nothing else there (its log goes to stderr). It needs no
daemon, socket or configuration. A Neovim configuration is in the
repository's editors/neovim/ directory.

== WHAT IT DOES
    diagnostics   textDocument/publishDiagnostics: exactly what -check
                  reports, errors and warnings, each at its range, with
                  its code; a module's in that module's file
    hover         textDocument/hover: a function's signature, a binding's
                  type, as the compiler shows them
    definition    textDocument/definition: the declaration's name, in the
                  program, its modules or the standard library
    references    textDocument/references: every reference to the same
                  binding in the analyzed program (by resolution, never
                  by spelling)
Documents are synchronized whole (didOpen, didChange, didSave, didClose):
an open document's unsaved text is what the compiler analyzes, and the
server never writes a file. Positions are UTF-16 (or UTF-32 when the
editor prefers it).

== PROGRAMS AND STALENESS
Each open document is analyzed as a program unless another open program
imports it; then it is that program's module. A document with a syntax
error is still analyzed, around its malformed source (error.surface-syntax,
"Checking around a syntax error"): the code the error cannot affect gets
fresh diagnostics, hover, definitions and references, and a position
whose meaning the malformed source may change is answered from the last
complete analysis where the text is unchanged, its hover marked stale, or
not at all. An analysis runs after a short pause in typing, in a worker
process, so the server answers while it runs.

== NOT YET
Completion, signature help, rename, code actions, formatting, semantic
tokens, workspace symbols and references across programs are not
implemented (LSP-MILESTONE-1.md, "Limitations").
